 is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\n$$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186335874 1074 "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ coincide with the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ and are given by the following expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is an integer." 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186335877 1071 "Find the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186335877 1071 "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186335877 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTo find the range of $f(x),$ we consider both intervals separately.\n\n- Multiplying the first interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\le -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\le -\\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second interval by $\\frac{2}{3}$ and subtracting $1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge 1 \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ge \\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})-1\\ge \\frac{2}{3}-1 \\\\  & f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining both inequalities, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)\\le -\\frac{5}{3},\\,f(x)\\ge -\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the range is $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{5}{3}]\\cup [-\\frac{1}{3},\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186335877 1074 "The range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ consists of two intervals:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can split this into two inequalities:\n\n$$\