e curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186345217 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ $\\,0\\le x\\le 4\\sqrt{6}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186345218 1071 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186345218 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186345220 1071 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186345220 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}({x}^{3}-2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the curve parametrized by $r(t)=4{t}^{2}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j$ for $0\\le t\\le 1.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186345224 1071 "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186345224 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ $\\,1\\le x\\le \\sqrt[4]{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186345228 1071 "Evaluate the line integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k\n$$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186345228 1071 "Evaluate the line integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k$\nfor $1<t<\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 false] [17592186345228 1074 "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $$\n\\displaystyle r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k,\n$$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =i+t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{3}^{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({5}^{3/2}-{3}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{5}-3\\sqrt{3}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539078 17592186576411 true] [17592186345228 1074 "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r(t)=t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,j+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=t,$ $y=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2},$ and $z=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =i+t\\,j+t\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=\\frac{y+z}{x}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}}{t}=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+2{t}^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=4t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{2}}t\\sqrt{1+2{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{3}^{5}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{3}^{5}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}